.Dt XTC_SLAB 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm xtc_slab_create ,
.Nm xtc_slab_destroy ,
.Nm xtc_slab_alloc ,
.Nm xtc_slab_free ,
.Nm xtc_slab_reap ,
.Nm xtc_slab_stat ,
.Nm xtc_slab_offset ,
.Nm xtc_slab_resolve ,
.Nm xtc_slab_pressure_create ,
.Nm xtc_slab_pressure_destroy ,
.Nm xtc_slab_reaper_spawn ,
.Nm xtc_slab_pressure_listen ,
.Nm xtc_slab_pressure_listen_ex ,
.Nm xtc_slab_pressure_stop ,
.Nm XTC_SLAB_OPTS_DEFAULT
.Nd slab + magazine allocator
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In xtc.h
.In xtc_slab.h
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_slab_create "const xtc_slab_opts_t *opts" "xtc_slab_t **out"
.Ft void
.Fn xtc_slab_destroy "xtc_slab_t *slab"
.Ft "void *"
.Fn xtc_slab_alloc "xtc_slab_t *slab"
.Ft void
.Fn xtc_slab_free "xtc_slab_t *slab" "void *obj"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_slab_reap "xtc_slab_t *slab"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_slab_stat "const xtc_slab_t *slab" "xtc_slab_stats_t *out"
.Ft xtc_slab_off_t
.Fn xtc_slab_offset "const xtc_slab_t *slab" "const void *p"
.Ft "void *"
.Fn xtc_slab_resolve "const xtc_slab_t *slab" "xtc_slab_off_t off"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_slab_pressure_create "const char *psi_path" "xtc_slab_pressure_fn fn" "void *user" "xtc_slab_pressure_t **out"
.Ft void
.Fn xtc_slab_pressure_destroy "xtc_slab_pressure_t *handle"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_slab_reaper_spawn "xtc_loop_t *loop" "int64_t interval_ns" "xtc_pid_t *out_pid"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm xtc_slab
is a libumem-style slab allocator with per-loop magazines.  Every
fixed-size hot-path allocation in xtc routes through it
.Pq lockmgr lock entries, proc link/monitor entries, RCU retired
.Pq nodes, timer nodes ;
applications can use it directly for any pool of same-sized
objects.
.Pp
A
.Vt slab
is a cache of objects of a single size.  Objects are carved from
.Va chunks
.Pq mmap'd regions, default 64 KiB ;
each chunk holds many slots.  Free objects are linked through
their first
.Va sizeof(void *)
bytes.
.Pp
.Sy Magazines :
each thread keeps a small per-cache cache
.Pq default 16 slots
of recently-freed objects.  Allocs hit the magazine
.Pq lock-free, no atomics ;
on miss the cache lock is taken and the magazine is refilled
from a partial chunk.  Frees push to the magazine; when full,
the magazine spills back to the cache under lock.
.Pp
.Sy Constructor / destructor :
optional callbacks run once per object lifetime.  Constructor on
the first time an object is pulled from a slot; destructor at
cache destroy or chunk reap.
.Pp
.Sy Storage modes:
.Bl -tag -width "XTC_SLAB_PROCESS_LOCAL" -offset indent
.It Dv XTC_SLAB_PROCESS_LOCAL
Default.  Chunks via
.Xr mmap 2
.Dv MAP_ANONYMOUS .
.It Dv XTC_SLAB_SHARED_MEMORY
Caller passes an mmap'd
.Dv MAP_SHARED
fd; the slab carves chunks from that region.  Object handles are
.Vt xtc_slab_off_t
offsets that two processes can resolve to local pointers using
.Fn xtc_slab_resolve .
The shared header at the start of the region holds an atomic
cursor so concurrent processes don't collide on chunk allocation.
.El
.Pp
.Sy Debug flags :
.Bl -tag -width "XTC_SLAB_BACKTRACE" -compact -offset indent
.It Dv XTC_SLAB_REDZONE
8-byte magic before+after every object; checked on free.
.It Dv XTC_SLAB_AUDIT
64-event ring of recent (alloc, free) events.
.It Dv XTC_SLAB_BACKTRACE
Backtrace each event
.Pq Linux/glibc only .
.It Dv XTC_SLAB_NO_MAGAZINE
Always go through the cache lock.  For diagnostic use.
.El
.Pp
.Sy OOM policies
control behaviour when no chunk can be allocated:
.Bl -tag -width "XTC_SLAB_OOM_BACKOFF" -compact -offset indent
.It Dv XTC_SLAB_OOM_FAIL
Return NULL.  Default.
.It Dv XTC_SLAB_OOM_BACKOFF
Brief usleep + retry once, then NULL.
.It Dv XTC_SLAB_OOM_ABORT
.Fn abort
.Pq debug builds .
.El
.Pp
.Fn xtc_slab_create
allocates a cache.
.Fa opts
is required and must set
.Fa opts->name
and
.Fa opts->obj_size ;
start from
.Dv XTC_SLAB_OPTS_INIT ,
which also sets
.Fa opts->size
to the caller's
.Fn sizeof .
The library reads no further than
.Fa opts->size
(later fields take their defaults) and returns
.Dv XTC_E_INVAL
when it does not cover
.Fa obj_size .
.Fn xtc_slab_stat
writes at most
.Fa out->size
bytes; initialize the stats with
.Dv XTC_SLAB_STATS_INIT .
.Pp
.Fn xtc_slab_alloc
returns a fresh object pointer or NULL on OOM.
.Fn xtc_slab_free
returns it to the cache.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_slab_reap
drops magazines and returns empty chunks to the OS.  Useful under
memory pressure.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_slab_stat
fills
.Fa out
with the cache's counters.  The per-operation counters
.Pq alloc_fast, alloc_slow, free_fast, free_slow
are kept per thread, on separate cache lines, and summed on read, so
threads allocating from one shared cache do not contend on a statistics
line;
.Va n_inuse
is derived from them.  The figures are exact when no alloc or free is in
flight; a read concurrent with other threads' traffic is a near-instant
snapshot.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_slab_pressure_create
attaches a callback that fires when Linux PSI memory pressure is
detected, and returns its handle in
.Fa out .  When triggered, all registered slab caches are reaped, then
.Fa fn Ns ( 2 , Fa user )
runs on the listener's own thread, never on a loop: it may block
briefly and must not park.
The trigger is
.Ql some 150000 2000000 :
150 ms of memory stall in a 2 s window, the shortest window the kernel
lets an unprivileged process use.
With
.Fa psi_path
.Dv NULL
the listener uses the calling process's cgroup v2
.Pa memory.pressure
(the pressure a
.Pa memory.max
or
.Pa memory.high
limit produces), and falls back to
.Pa /proc/pressure/memory ;
an explicit path is used alone.
.Fn xtc_slab_pressure_destroy
stops the listener and frees the handle; a listener meant to run until
exit keeps its handle.
.Fn xtc_slab_pressure_create
returns
.Dv XTC_E_NOSYS
only without PSI (no such file, or not Linux),
.Dv XTC_E_INVAL
when the kernel refuses the trigger, and
.Dv XTC_E_IO
when the file cannot be opened for writing (no permission, or not a
PSI file).
.Pp
.Fn xtc_slab_reaper_spawn
spawns an
.Xr xtc_proc 3
that periodically calls
.Fn xtc_slab_reap_all
.Pq returns total objects reaped .
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
struct widget { int id; char name[32]; };

xtc_slab_opts_t opts = XTC_SLAB_OPTS_INIT;
opts.name = "widgets";
opts.obj_size = sizeof(struct widget);
opts.flags = XTC_SLAB_REDZONE | XTC_SLAB_AUDIT;

xtc_slab_t *slab;
xtc_slab_create(&opts, &slab);

struct widget *w = xtc_slab_alloc(slab);
w->id = 42;
strcpy(w->name, "answer");

xtc_slab_free(slab, w);
xtc_slab_destroy(slab);
.Ed
.Sh DEPRECATED
These names still work through the rest of 2.x and are
removed in 3.0; see
.Lk https://codeberg.org/gregburd/libxtc/src/branch/main/docs/deprecations.md docs/deprecations.md .
A rebuild that uses one warns.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fn xtc_slab_pressure_listen
Use
.Fn xtc_slab_pressure_create ;
the handle is now returned: pass an out pointer and keep (or never destroy) it.
.It Fn xtc_slab_pressure_listen_ex
Use
.Fn xtc_slab_pressure_create ;
same arguments.
.It Fn xtc_slab_pressure_stop
Use
.Fn xtc_slab_pressure_destroy ;
the new name returns void; the old one returns XTC_E_INVAL for NULL, XTC_E_NOSYS without PSI, else XTC_OK.
.It Dv XTC_SLAB_OPTS_DEFAULT
Use
.Dv XTC_SLAB_OPTS_INIT .
.El
